00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this morning on the dish a trip to a  place where food is usually frowned upon  the museum but that is hardly the case  at a relatively new institution in  brooklyn new york  the museum of food and drink was founded  to showcase culinary traditions from  across the globe while  educating visitors about the cultures  they come from  and now mofad is facing a new challenge  to continue its mission  in the virtual world this is the most  amount i've ever cooked in my home  ever it was the perfect pairing an  online food conversation and  demonstration with famed israeli-born  chef michael salmanov  and his friend and fellow cookbook  author now based in israel  adina sussman it might have been like 2  a.m for her  in all fairness whenever we hang out  together in tel aviv it's usually 2  o'clock in the morning anyways  their hangout this time had a few more  involved let's talk about spices  including a virtual audience to have  something that  brings a little bit of purpose and sort  of brings it back to what we do in our  life that matters the people in our life  that matter  you know that was like really the  opportunity food is something at least  right now that's still bringing us  together isn't it  food definitely is and i think we have  this opportunity to have these  sort of interactions that are like in a  way public but also span different  continents at the same time  israeli pantry staples as the program  was called was put together by the  museum of food and drink  they talked about you know quarantine  cooking and their middle eastern pantry  staples  and chef mike demonstrated his  five-minute hummus so what we're going  to make first is a cleaner sauce  and we're going to spin it hey everyone  mike is doing his thing with the food  processor siri cayman is the program  director for the five-year-old museum of  food and drink or mofad  oh that looks good its goal using food  to educate  inspire and bring people together the  purpose is to show that  food is culture it's not just about  cooking classes and it's not just about  eating at restaurants we are really  interested in  telling the stories of food sharing the  history  contextualizing food mofad started as a  mobile exhibition  expanded into a 5 000 square foot  interactive space in brooklyn  and then while in the process of  relocating came  covid 19. so how did you guys pivot  once the pandemic hit and you knew you  had to do something different  it was very challenging and really  overwhelming my concern at first  everybody was pivoting to online and  there was so much content happening  and then it just became apparent that  actually this was an opportunity  to even grow our community once you  reopen like as operators what what  what changes are going to come first i  mean it's  impossible to say really mofad first  looked within its own community  giving an amplified online voice to  those in the industry looking to help  others  like greg backstrom of olmsted in  brooklyn they were  kind enough to put us on their platform  to help inform people  to spread the word that we have a food  bank and that if anyone needs it you  know to come  you said you learned also from it what  did you learn in that  in the experience well just you know  that we're not alone that there's a lot  of support there's a lot of good will  out there  you want to peel your peers so it should  look something like this by connecting  food creators and food lovers from  around the world virtually  mofad expanded its base in ways never  envisioned  all visitors need a computer or  smartphone and they're transported to  online cooking classes  you can see that it's not the sauce  sitting on top of the pasta and  even dinner parties we're giving them  recipes we're giving them boxes of  ingredients they can order  a way to sort of cook together even if  we're not together and then at least we  all sit down  at the same time and connect online it's  in  those moments that mofad found food can  also be a gateway to conversation about  the issues of the day  this is a new series like racism against  asian americans during the pandemic  so we've had a couple different panel  conversations sort of contextualizing it  to food so we've talked about chinese  restaurants the survival of restaurants  and chinatowns  food taboos as they relate to xenophobia  and our own cultural bias  but what have you found in some of these  conversations you know i think it's  difficult  the younger restaurateurs are coming in  and trying to give support and help  these older restaurants  find means to innovate and strategize  right now so that they don't have to  shut their doors but  it's a really difficult situation we use  food as a lens to understand  ourselves each other and the world  around us catherine pickley is the  acting president of the museum of food  and drink  food also is something that brings  people together of different backgrounds  how important is that right now i think  that's incredibly important i'm going to  butcher this quote but  i think that leah chase said something  along the lines of you know we all  gather together  at the table and we talk and we can  change the world  i think that's really really true that's  the power of food  a power that even before the black lives  matter's protests began  mofad hoped would help tell the story of  the african-american contribution to our  country's food history  its exhibit african american making the  nation's table  had its opening postponed by the  pandemic we  are exploring ways that we can open the  exhibition virtually  you know how do we get this larger  community than we ever had before  able to really see these stories and  begin to  uncover history that was lost  or not told mofad is at its heart a  little bit of an activist organization  it's uncovering these inequities and  saying come together come together  around this thing that you love  should we be reheating call it mofad's  recipe for success from the very start  food is culture and ancestry and  advocacy and it's where you come from  it's part of what you are it's your  memories it's conflict it's commonality  it's all those things  so obviously a museum sort of to  celebrate that or to have a discussion  about that is is  of the utmost importance and i know we  said it a lot in the piece but it's the  thing that brings us together and it is  a place where you can have difficult  conversations because you are gathering  around it can't bring us together in the  same way inside restaurants right now  unfortunately but it was fun to look at  all the food we're both hungry  and to talk about celebrating food

